      "authored_at": "2026-08-22T14:40:01+05:30",
      "subject": "ClassMind V1: verify the end-to-end workflow, and fix what verifying it exposed",
      "body": "The product platform built up to this point had never been driven. Lint, tsc\nand build all passed, which says nothing about whether a faculty member can get\nfrom an upload to a student reading a confirmed deadline. Two things were\nneeded to find out, and running it found a real defect.\n\n**A fixture transcription provider** (`src/lib/transcription/fixture.ts`),\nselected by `TRANSCRIPTION_PROVIDER=fixture`. It replays three verbatim Sarvam\nBatch responses captured during Lab v0 RUN 1 and exported once to\n`fixtures/transcription/` -- the product never reads Lab v0's `runs` table. The\nresponse bytes are real, so normalization, evidence offsets and provenance are\nall exercised against a genuine transcript rather than against a synthesised\none; only the network call and the queue delay are simulated. Sarvam stays the\ndefault so nothing can fall back to replay by accident, and every provenance\nrecord written this way says REPLAYED, NOT TRANSCRIBED in its limitations.\n\n**An end-to-end driver** (`scripts/e2e.mts`) that drives the running server over\nHTTP exactly as a browser does: sign in, create a course, add context, upload\n10.5 MB of real audio through the signed URL, transcribe, poll, extract,\nconfirm/edit/reject, then re-enter as a student and ask a question. 67 checks,\n0 failures. The load-bearing ones are negative: a student's lecture payload\ncarries zero candidates, a student cannot rule on one or trigger extraction,\nand the anon key cannot read any of the four tables directly.\n\n**The defect it exposed.** The rules engine read an 18-minute university\ncourse-outline lecture as 2 generic guidance items. It missed the entire\nmodule-by-module syllabus and the prescribed textbook -- the only two things in\nthat lecture a student actually needs. The lexicon was built from a Class-12\ncoaching lecture, so it knew \"homework\", \"DPP\" and \"notes on Telegram\" and had\nno rule for a lecturer walking through a syllabus.\n\nFixed by adding two rules, both at suppression \"none\" because every sentence\nthey exist to catch is first-person-plural and veto A would delete all of them:\n\n  exam_scope.course_coverage      gated on a curricular unit noun, so\n                                  \"we will look at the compressor\" stays silent\n                                  while \"in the second module we discuss...\"\n                                  fires\n  announcement.prescribed_material  a named textbook plus a prescription cue,\n                                  so \"buy this\" is separated from \"a book exists\"\n\nSame lecture now yields 21 candidates. Method version bumped 1.0.0 -> 1.1.0:\na stored candidate names the version that produced it, and two rule sets\nsharing one version string would make every earlier accuracy number unreadable.\n\nExtraction suite 65 -> 75 cases, including the negative gates.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
      "is_auto_save": false
    }
